-- MRCP Part 2: Palliative medicine and end of life care -- batch 1
-- 50 questions: end-of-life-decision-making (13), opioid-conversion-and-syringe-drivers (13),
-- pain-management (12), symptom-control (12)

  'draft', 'core',
  'An 82-year-old woman with metastatic pancreatic cancer and liver metastases is admitted with worsening jaundice and confusion. She has no advance decision or lasting power of attorney. The palliative care team assesses that she lacks capacity to make decisions about further chemotherapy or escalation of care due to hepatic encephalopathy. Her two adult children disagree: one wants continued active treatment, the other wants a comfort-focused approach.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in decision-making?',
  array['best interests decision','Mental Capacity Act','family disagreement']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 76-year-old man with severe COPD (FEV1 22% predicted), cor pulmonale and recurrent admissions for type 2 respiratory failure is admitted with pneumonia. He retains capacity. The consultant explains that if he were to have a cardiac arrest, CPR would be very unlikely to restart his heart effectively given his severe underlying disease, and even if it did, he would likely need prolonged ventilation with a very poor chance of returning to his previous function. Despite this, the patient states he wants CPR attempted if his heart stops.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to the DNACPR decision?',
  array['DNACPR','CPR futility','shared decision-making']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 68-year-old woman with motor neurone disease has an advance decision to refuse treatment (ADRT), completed 18 months ago while she had capacity, stating that she does not wish to be started on invasive or non-invasive ventilation 'if my breathing fails due to my MND.' She now presents in type 2 respiratory failure secondary to a chest infection superimposed on her progressive respiratory muscle weakness, and has since lost capacity to communicate due to severe bulbar dysfunction.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate approach to her ADRT?',
  array['advance decision to refuse treatment','motor neurone disease','validity and applicability']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 90-year-old man with advanced dementia and recurrent aspiration pneumonia is admitted following a further aspiration event, now with worsening hypoxia and reduced consciousness. He lacks capacity to decide about further treatment. His son holds a registered Lasting Power of Attorney for health and welfare and insists on full escalation, including intensive care admission and mechanical ventilation, stating 'he would want everything done.' The medical and palliative care teams assess that ICU admission and ventilation would not be in his best interests given his severe frailty, advanced dementia, and poor prognosis.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['Lasting Power of Attorney','best interests','escalation of care dispute']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 55-year-old woman with a grade 4 glioblastoma is admitted with worsening headache and drowsiness. Her capacity to consent to a discussion about ceiling of care fluctuates through the day: in the morning she is drowsy and confused, but by early afternoon after a dose of dexamethasone she is alert and able to engage clearly in conversation about her prognosis and preferences.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to assessing her capacity for this decision?',
  array['fluctuating capacity','Mental Capacity Act','glioblastoma']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 45-year-old man with decompensated alcohol-related cirrhosis and type 1 hepatorenal syndrome has been on renal replacement therapy for two weeks with no improvement in liver or renal function despite treatment with terlipressin and albumin. He is not a transplant candidate due to ongoing alcohol use. The nephrology and hepatology teams believe continuing dialysis is now futile and are considering stopping it, but his wife insists dialysis must continue 'to give him a chance.'$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['withdrawal of dialysis','futile treatment','family conflict']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$An 84-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer and known vertebral bone metastases develops acute paraparesis and urinary retention. MRI confirms malignant spinal cord compression above a background of widespread visceral metastatic disease with an estimated prognosis of weeks. The oncology and palliative care teams agree that, given her overall disease burden and functional status, she is unlikely to benefit meaningfully from surgical decompression or radiotherapy in terms of regaining function, and that the focus should shift to comfort. Her family, upset by the sudden decline, insist 'everything possible' must be done, including surgery.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate approach to this conflict?',
  array['family conflict','goals of care','honest communication']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 60-year-old man with laryngeal cancer and a tracheostomy has intractable breathlessness and distress from tumour progression despite optimised opioids, benzodiazepines, and other measures. He retains capacity and, understanding that it may hasten his death, requests deep continuous sedation to relieve his suffering in his last days of life.',
  'Which of the following best describes the ethical and legal distinction between the requested proportionate palliative sedation and euthanasia?',
  array['palliative sedation','doctrine of double effect','refractory symptoms']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 77-year-old woman with severe advanced dementia, complete dependency for all activities of daily living, and recurrent aspiration pneumonia over the past six months is admitted with a further aspiration event. She lacks capacity, has no LPA, and her family (who all agree) feel that continued hospital admissions, antibiotics and clinically assisted nutrition and hydration are prolonging suffering rather than benefiting her, consistent with what they believe she would have wanted. The MDT concurs that a comfort-focused approach, without further antibiotics or artificial hydration, is in her best interests.',
  'Given that the family and the treating team agree on withdrawing clinically assisted nutrition and hydration, what is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['CANH withdrawal','An NHS Trust v Y','best interests']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 70-year-old man with motor neurone disease, dependent on long-term nocturnal and increasingly daytime non-invasive ventilation (NIV) for respiratory muscle weakness, tells his palliative care team and neurologist, while retaining full capacity, that he wishes to stop NIV because his quality of life has become intolerable, understanding this will lead to his death within hours to days.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to withdrawing NIV in this situation?',
  array['withdrawal of ventilation','terminal wean','patient autonomy']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 58-year-old woman with recurrent ovarian cancer and malignant bowel obstruction is admitted for symptom control. She retains capacity and clearly states she would not want CPR attempted if her heart were to stop, given her poor prognosis. Her husband, present at the discussion, becomes distressed and insists 'we are not giving up -- she must be for full resuscitation.'$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate approach to documenting her resuscitation status?',
  array['patient autonomy','DNACPR','family distress']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 82-year-old man with metastatic gastric cancer, having completed two lines of palliative chemotherapy with ongoing disease progression and worsening performance status, retains full capacity. After a detailed discussion of the risks, limited likely benefit, and burden of further chemotherapy, he clearly states he does not want further chemotherapy or escalation of care, preferring to focus on quality time at home with his family.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['patient autonomy','advance care planning','stopping active treatment']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013', 'mrcp2', '88deb7cd-4cc0-43cb-8036-896c04d2f1c0',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 66-year-old woman with end-stage renal failure on a background of multiple myeloma is in the last days of life on the renal ward, with a background of a hypoactive delirium that fluctuates through the day. During lucid periods she has previously stated she would not want IV fluids 'if things got to this stage,' but during confused periods she repeatedly asks nursing staff for water and appears distressed when it is limited. Her family are anxious that she is being 'left to dehydrate.'$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate approach to hydration in her last days of life?',
  array['clinically assisted hydration','last days of life','family communication']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 65-year-old man with oesophageal cancer causing near-complete dysphagia is established on oral morphine sulfate modified-release 60mg twice daily (120mg/24h total), with good pain control but now unable to swallow tablets. The palliative care team plans to convert him to a continuous subcutaneous infusion via syringe driver using diamorphine.',
  'What is the most appropriate diamorphine dose to prescribe in the syringe driver over 24 hours?',
  array['opioid conversion','diamorphine','syringe driver']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 58-year-old woman with recurrent ovarian cancer and malignant bowel obstruction is vomiting persistently and can no longer reliably absorb oral medication. She has been taking oral morphine sulfate immediate-release 20mg six times daily (120mg/24h) with adequate analgesia. The team plans to convert her to a continuous subcutaneous infusion of morphine sulfate via syringe driver, as diamorphine is unavailable at this site.',
  'What is the most appropriate subcutaneous morphine dose to prescribe over 24 hours?',
  array['opioid conversion','subcutaneous morphine','syringe driver']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 72-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer and bone pain has been taking oral oxycodone modified-release 40mg twice daily (80mg/24h) with good effect but has developed dysphagia due to disease progression and requires conversion to a subcutaneous oxycodone infusion via syringe driver.',
  'What is the most appropriate approximate subcutaneous oxycodone dose to prescribe over 24 hours?',
  array['opioid conversion','oxycodone','syringe driver']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000017', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 69-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer has been using a transdermal fentanyl patch 50 micrograms/hour, changed every 72 hours, with good analgesic control. She develops a paralytic ileus and profound vomiting, and the palliative care team decides to remove the patch and convert to a subcutaneous diamorphine infusion via syringe driver, given ongoing concerns about erratic transdermal absorption from poor peripheral perfusion.',
  'In addition to calculating the equivalent diamorphine dose, what is the most important practical consideration when making this switch?',
  array['fentanyl patch','opioid switching','syringe driver']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000018', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 74-year-old man with metastatic bladder cancer has just had his syringe driver dose increased to 90mg diamorphine subcutaneously over 24 hours for worsening background pain, having previously been well controlled on a lower dose.',
  'What is the most appropriate breakthrough (as-required) subcutaneous diamorphine dose to prescribe for episodes of pain alongside this new driver rate?',
  array['breakthrough dose','diamorphine','syringe driver']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000019', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 79-year-old man with metastatic renal cell carcinoma and an acute-on-chronic kidney injury (eGFR now 12 mL/min/1.73m2, previously 45) has been on a stable subcutaneous morphine infusion for bone pain. Over the past 24 hours, his family report increasing drowsiness, visual hallucinations, and jerking movements of his limbs, without evidence of new sepsis or intracranial pathology on assessment.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management of his symptoms?',
  array['opioid-induced neurotoxicity','renal impairment','opioid switching']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000020', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A palliative care team is preparing a syringe driver for a 71-year-old woman with ovarian cancer who requires diamorphine for pain, cyclizine for nausea, and dexamethasone for tumour-related oedema, all planned to run together in the same subcutaneous infusion.',
  'What is the most important compatibility concern with this combination?',
  array['syringe driver compatibility','dexamethasone','cyclizine']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 63-year-old man with widespread metastatic pancreatic cancer has poorly controlled pain despite escalating doses of a strong opioid, with increasing concerns about opioid-induced hyperalgesia. The palliative care consultant proposes switching him to methadone as a third-line opioid, to be initiated and titrated under specialist palliative care supervision.',
  'Which of the following best describes an important pharmacological consideration specific to converting a patient to methadone?',
  array['methadone','opioid switching','non-linear conversion']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000022', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 67-year-old woman with metastatic cervical cancer has been established on a transdermal buprenorphine patch for background pain but requires escalation because of worsening pelvic pain from local tumour progression, and the team wishes to switch her to oral morphine for easier titration.',
  'What is the most important practical point to consider when switching this patient from a buprenorphine patch to oral morphine?',
  array['buprenorphine patch','opioid switching','transdermal opioids']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000023', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 75-year-old man with metastatic lung cancer on a stable subcutaneous diamorphine syringe driver develops increasing agitation, vivid visual hallucinations and multifocal myoclonus over 48 hours, without evidence of infection, hypercalcaemia, or new intracranial disease on assessment. His current driver dose has recently been escalated twice in quick succession for reported 'breakthrough pain.'$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['opioid-induced neurotoxicity','myoclonus','dose escalation']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000024', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 66-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer has achieved good pain control on oral morphine sulfate modified-release 90mg twice daily (180mg/24h) but is now struggling to swallow tablets reliably due to oropharyngeal tumour extension, though she can still tolerate small amounts of oral liquid. The team decides to convert her to a transdermal fentanyl patch for convenience and to reduce swallowing burden.',
  'Using standard published conversion tables, which of the following most closely represents the equivalent fentanyl patch strength for her total 24-hour oral morphine dose?',
  array['fentanyl patch','opioid conversion','transdermal opioids']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000025', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A palliative care team is setting up a syringe driver for a 70-year-old man with metastatic colorectal cancer who has pain (controlled with diamorphine), nausea, and increasing terminal agitation, for which the team plans to add haloperidol and midazolam to the same infusion alongside the diamorphine.',
  'Which of the following is the most appropriate consideration regarding this three-drug combination?',
  array['syringe driver compatibility','midazolam','haloperidol']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000026', 'mrcp2', '50bd47d2-da40-489a-959e-977e9ce5d9af',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 61-year-old man with metastatic head and neck cancer has been stable on a subcutaneous diamorphine syringe driver at 60mg/24h. Over the past two days, his community palliative care nurse reports he has required five breakthrough doses of 10mg subcutaneous diamorphine daily for ongoing background pain, in addition to the driver, with good relief each time and no adverse effects.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in managing his syringe driver?',
  array['breakthrough dose','syringe driver titration','dose review']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000027', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 59-year-old man with locally advanced unresectable pancreatic cancer has severe, constant epigastric and back pain radiating in a band-like distribution, poorly responsive to a rapidly escalating strong opioid (now equivalent to over 300mg oral morphine per day) combined with adjuvant analgesics, and he is experiencing increasing sedation and constipation from the opioid dose without adequate pain relief.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in managing his pain?',
  array['coeliac plexus block','visceral pain','pancreatic cancer']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000028', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 63-year-old woman with non-small cell lung cancer has tumour infiltration of the brachial plexus causing severe burning, shooting pain radiating down her arm with associated allodynia, poorly responsive to a strong opioid alone despite dose escalation.',
  'What is the most appropriate adjuvant analgesic to add for this pain?',
  array['neuropathic pain','brachial plexus invasion','adjuvant analgesia']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000029', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 74-year-old man with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer and multiple lytic bone metastases in the lumbar spine and pelvis reports well-localised, worsening pain at these sites, only partially controlled with a strong opioid and regular paracetamol, without evidence of spinal cord compression on recent imaging.',
  'In addition to optimising systemic analgesia, which of the following is most likely to provide effective, targeted relief of this localised bone pain?',
  array['bone metastases','radiotherapy','prostate cancer']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000030', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 70-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer and known thoracic vertebral bone metastases develops sudden worsening mid-back pain with new bilateral leg weakness and a sensory level at the umbilicus. She has been on a stable opioid regimen for background bone pain.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate step in management?',
  array['malignant spinal cord compression','oncological emergency','dexamethasone']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000031', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 56-year-old man with metastatic renal cell carcinoma has stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 18 mL/min/1.73m2) and worsening bone pain from pelvic metastases, currently managed with regular paracetamol and a low-dose strong opioid, with inadequate control despite recent dose increases and increasing somnolence.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step regarding his opioid regimen given his renal function?',
  array['renal impairment','opioid choice','bone pain']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000032', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 68-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer and extensive lumbar vertebral metastases reports that her background pain is generally well controlled on her current opioid regimen, but she experiences severe, sharp pain specifically when she attempts to stand up or turn in bed, lasting a few minutes and then settling.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to managing this specific pain pattern?',
  array['incident pain','breakthrough analgesia','bone metastases']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000033', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 61-year-old man with advanced laryngeal cancer, significant psychosocial distress related to unresolved family conflict, financial worries, and spiritual distress about the meaning of his illness, reports pain that remains poorly controlled despite appropriate escalation of opioids and adjuvant analgesics, with a normal physical examination and no evidence of a new pain-generating lesion on recent imaging.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in his pain management?',
  array['total pain','holistic care','refractory pain']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000034', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 65-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer and severe neuropathic pain from brachial plexus invasion has failed to achieve adequate relief despite optimised opioids, a trial of amitriptyline, and pregabalin at maximal tolerated doses, with ongoing severe burning pain significantly affecting her quality of life.',
  'Which of the following is the most appropriate next pharmacological option to consider for her refractory neuropathic pain?',
  array['ketamine','refractory neuropathic pain','adjuvant analgesia']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000035', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 57-year-old man with metastatic sarcoma and diffuse, severe pain that has failed to respond adequately to optimised systemic opioids (with intolerable sedation limiting further dose increase), multiple adjuvants, and opioid switching, is being considered by the palliative care and anaesthetic pain teams for further intervention.',
  'Which of the following is the most appropriate advanced technique to consider for his refractory pain?',
  array['intrathecal analgesia','refractory pain','interventional pain management']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000036', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 66-year-old man with locally advanced rectal cancer reports a persistent, distressing sensation of needing to defecate, with severe rectal and perineal pain, despite having minimal actual stool passed and no evidence of complete bowel obstruction on recent assessment.',
  'What is the most appropriate description and initial management approach for this symptom?',
  array['tenesmus','rectal cancer','local tumour infiltration']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000037', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 78-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer and multiple bone metastases has moderate bone pain, currently managed with regular paracetamol and a weak opioid, and the team is considering adding a regular NSAID for additional analgesic effect. He has a background of stage 3 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2) and a prior peptic ulcer that healed after Helicobacter pylori eradication five years ago.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to using an NSAID in this context?',
  array['NSAID risk-benefit','bone pain','renal impairment']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000038', 'mrcp2', 'dc7fb0a6-9eef-472f-958d-2c53ed41a85d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 52-year-old woman with metastatic cholangiocarcinoma and multiple liver metastases develops worsening right upper quadrant and right shoulder-tip pain, described as a constant dull ache with intermittent sharp exacerbations, related to rapid recent growth of her liver metastases confirmed on imaging, despite an already optimised opioid regimen.',
  'What is the most appropriate additional treatment to specifically target this pain mechanism?',
  array['hepatic capsular pain','corticosteroids','liver metastases']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000039', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 66-year-old woman with recurrent ovarian cancer and known peritoneal carcinomatosis presents with colicky abdominal pain, distension, and vomiting. CT confirms multiple transition points consistent with malignant bowel obstruction. Given the diffuse peritoneal disease, surgery and a venting gastrostomy are not considered appropriate options by the surgical and oncology teams, and a conservative medical approach is planned.',
  'Which combination of medications is most appropriate for the initial conservative management of her symptoms?',
  array['malignant bowel obstruction','octreotide','hyoscine butylbromide']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000040', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 61-year-old man with metastatic melanoma and known brain metastases develops worsening nausea and vomiting, described by him as worse in the mornings and associated with a mild new headache, without an obvious metabolic or drug-related cause identified on review of his medications and blood tests.',
  'Which antiemetic is most appropriate as first-line treatment, given the likely mechanism of his nausea?',
  array['raised intracranial pressure','cyclizine','antiemetic choice']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000041', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$An 84-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer is in the last days of life, now unconscious and unable to swallow, and develops noisy, rattling respiratory secretions with each breath ('death rattle'), which is not causing him visible distress but is greatly upsetting his family at the bedside.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate approach to managing this symptom?',
  array['death rattle','last days of life','hyoscine']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000042', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 70-year-old man with severe COPD and superimposed lung cancer has persistent, distressing breathlessness at rest despite optimised inhaled therapy, home oxygen where indicated, and treatment of any reversible contributing factors (no current infection, effusion, or anaemia). He also describes significant associated anxiety when breathless.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in managing his breathlessness?',
  array['refractory breathlessness','low-dose opioids','handheld fan']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000043', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 75-year-old woman with advanced dementia and metastatic breast cancer, currently in the last days of life, becomes increasingly restless and agitated overnight, pulling at her bedclothes and calling out, despite her background pain and nausea both previously being well controlled on a syringe driver. On examination she has a palpably distended bladder and has not passed urine for over 12 hours.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management step?',
  array['terminal agitation','urinary retention','reversible causes']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000044', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 59-year-old woman with recurrent ovarian cancer and refractory malignant ascites has required increasingly frequent large-volume paracentesis every 7-10 days over the past two months for symptomatic relief of abdominal distension and breathlessness, each time requiring hospital attendance, with a reducing symptom-free interval between drainages.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step to improve her quality of life and reduce hospital attendances?',
  array['malignant ascites','indwelling peritoneal catheter','recurrent paracentesis']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000045', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 67-year-old man with cholangiocarcinoma and biliary obstruction from tumour invasion (not amenable to further stenting, having already failed two attempts) develops severe, generalised pruritus with a rising bilirubin, significantly affecting his sleep and quality of life. A trial of cholestyramine has provided no benefit.',
  'Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in managing his pruritus?',
  array['cholestatic pruritus','rifampicin','sertraline']
),
('db000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000046', 'mrcp2', '8b46a57d-df45-41cf-b4b0-b71fc7566ab8',
